In `_exclude_ipv4_networks`, `networks` and `networks_to_exclude` are parameters defined at function entry. The function first reads `networks_to_exclude` at the `sort(...)` call and again in the `for network_to_exclude in networks_to_exclude` loop header. Each loop iteration defines `network_to_exclude`, and that value is read inside the nested helper at `address_exclude(network_to_exclude)` and, on the exception path, at `network.overlaps(network_to_exclude)`. Inside the loop, `networks` is read in `map(_exclude_ipv4_network, networks)`, then `nested_networks` is defined by that assignment and read immediately in the flattening comprehension; that same comprehension also redefines `networks`, whose final value is later read by `return networks`. Because the loop runs twice, the second iteration uses the redefined `networks` from the previous iteration when evaluating the next `map(...)`.

